---
description: >
  Brevity-sweep audit of `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` — 1,940 lines / 107KB. Description ~1,685 chars (8x cap), intent ~532 words (5x cap), 15 desired outcomes, body Strategy with ~12 decisions. Among the worst-bloated leaves; saturated with marker tokens, type signatures, and parser implementation contracts.
intent: >
  parseBrainAide is the canonical reader for `.aide/config/brain.aide`. The
  spec balloons because every named body section, every marker token pair,
  every typed-result variant, every retired-field rejection rule, and every
  substitution-surface partition has its own outcome. Frontmatter and body
  both saturated with code-level invariants. Wholesale rewrite required.
misalignment:
  - spec-bloat
---

## Issues

- [ ] `description` is ~1,685 chars (cap 200) — packs the parser scope, the
  two-field frontmatter schema, the six body sections by name, the marker
  token format spec, the typed-result discriminant, and the never-throws
  invariant. Trim to ≤200 chars naming the parser's domain purpose.
  Traces to: `description` field | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content (marker tokens, exact strings, type signatures)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Forbidden-content

- [ ] `intent` is ~532 words (cap 100). Restates the parser's contract end-to-end:
  source-of-truth file, frontmatter schema, body marker grammar, tagged-result
  variants, interpolation surface partition, no-throws invariant, deprecated-
  fields rejection, retired marker grammar rejection. Trim to ≤100 words.
  Traces to: `intent` field | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Forbidden-content

- [ ] `outcomes.desired` has 15 items (cap 4); each is paragraph length and
  encodes implementation contracts: every named body section is its own
  field on the parsed result, marker tokens are recognized lowercase and
  case-sensitive, deprecated fields rejected by name, result variants
  enumerated. Most are unit-test-territory.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired` | Brevity Contract caps + outcomes-purpose test
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Forbidden-content

- [ ] `outcomes.undesired` has ~12 paragraph-length items, almost all citing
  retired schema names (`connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `tools`),
  retired marker grammars (`aide-prose-*`, `aide-playbook-*`, `aide-research-*`,
  three-section pre-amendment, four-section pre-rework), retired sentinel
  designs. Forbidden content (retired prior designs, migration history).
  Traces to: `outcomes.undesired` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

- [ ] `## Context` is ~700+ words (cap ~150). Recapitulates the parser's role,
  the consumer roster, the marker grammar history, the typed-key boundary,
  the substitution-surface partition.
  Traces to: `## Context` | Brevity Contract caps + Sibling-redundancy
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Sibling-redundancy

- [ ] `## Strategy` has ~12 paragraph-length decisions (cap ~5 ≤80 words). Each
  encodes implementation contracts: which fields land on the result variant,
  which markers populate which keys, the strict-failure rejection rule for
  each retired pattern, the kebab-to-camel-case mapping rule.
  Traces to: `## Strategy` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Forbidden-content

- [ ] `## Good examples` and `## Bad examples` reproduce TypeScript code
  showing exact `parseBrainAide` return shapes, exact YAML frontmatter
  shapes with all six section markers, exact deprecated-field rejection
  messages. Cap is 2-3 brief examples; forbidden content saturated.
  Traces to: examples sections | Forbidden content (TypeScript code, type signatures, exact strings, marker tokens)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

- [ ] Sibling redundancy with project root and brain-related submodule specs:
  every named body section's existence, the live-vs-seed split, the no-substitution-on-body-sections
  rule, the closed-vocabulary rejection rules — all restated verbatim from
  parents.
  Traces to: outcomes, Strategy, Bad examples | Sibling-redundancy
  Misalignment: spec-gap
  Category: Sibling-redundancy

## Retro

parseBrainAide is the textbook example of a parser spec that became a code
contract document. The right scope for this spec is "the canonical reader
for the user-owned brain config; returns a tagged-result variant; never
throws; preserves null in args verbatim; rejects retired schemas with a
specific reason." Everything else — exact field names, exact marker tokens,
exact rejection rule shapes — lives in code with focused unit tests asserting
the rejection behavior.

After the brain-layer parent is introduced, this spec shrinks to ≤100 lines
total. The 12+ retired-pattern Bad examples are git history.
